  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1000 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nMonday night through Friday:\n\nThe aforementioned high pressure shifts into the Ohio Valley \ninto Tuesday. Meanwhile, a positively-tilted upper trough digs \neast from Nevada into Utah, allowing diffluent upper-level flow \nto overspread the Great Plains and form broad low pressure at \nthe surface. Southerly WAA will develop between the two pressure\nsystems Tuesday, driving rain chances that build west to east \ninto southern WI through the day. Widespread 50-65% rain chances\nare then forecast into Tuesday night (due to potential for a \nLLJ to form overhead and reinforce the rain showers). Instability\nis looking very limited for now, but a few rumbles of thunder \ncan't be ruled out Tuesday night. Some of this rain could then \nlinger into Wednesday morning. \n\nFrom midday Wednesday onwards, predictability remains very low\nat this time; ensembles members continue to disagree on whether\nthe aforementioned upper trough will remain an open wave (and \ntrack faster) or close off into a pseudo \"upper low\" (and track \nslower). Even amongst ensemble members that agree on the phase\nof the trough, there is considerable variability on the track of\nany attendant surface low pressure. 500mb cluster analysis\nreveal even greater ensemble dispersion regarding the longwave\ntrough/ridge pattern of the polar jet along the US / Canada\nborder. In other words, the central CONUS as a whole is in for \nactive weather (rain and thunder) mid to late next week, but \nwhether or not that specifically includes southern WI (or sets \nup further south) is to be determined. On the bright side, thunder\npotential can generally be limited to \"slight chance\" in the\nforecast for now, given that GEFS instability plumes are \npredominantly centered well south of our region. \n\nSheppard",
